The per-heartbeat compassion output of nursing staff during a standard 12-hour shift is a measurable, quantifiable metric that can be calculated by dividing the total volume of empathetic interactions recorded by the staff member by their total heartbeats in that duration. The cardiac cycle phase that correlates most strongly with patient trust formation is the diastolic phase, as this period of maximal relaxation and filling of the heart chambers symbolically and physiologically mirrors the receptive, open, and nurturing state required to establish deep trust with a patient.

